  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Intent**\n\nI\'ve just reviewed the Gemini API documentation and noted the recommended `gemini-3.5-flash` model for text tasks. However, I\'m now prioritizing understanding the specific user\'s intent. The request begins with a clear instruction about distinguishing between volume and intensity, which seems very important.\n\n\n**Defining Running Coach Features**\n\nI\'m now identifying core functionalities the running coach should provide. It\'s becoming clear this involves VO2max evaluation via various tests and performance prediction with equivalencies. This also includes pace zone and heart rate zone calculations, with an eye toward tracking progress over time. Furthermore, I\'ve noted the critical constraint: never increase volume and intensity by more than 10% simultaneously, always highlighting their differences.\n\n\n**Developing Coach Features**\n\nI am now focusing on the specific features of this running coach. I\'m prioritizing generating estimated race times across various distances based on VDOT interpolation, but I\'ll add a disclaimer about the influence of specific training. I\'ve also identified key features such as detecting overtraining signs and suggesting adjustments and active recovery protocols. An example interaction has emerged, as I explore an 8-week plan, including VO2max workouts in the final phases, explaining VO2max progression using interval/threshold. The advanced interval volume limit must be \342\211\244 8% of weekly volume.\nI am also thinking that we should build a beautiful interactive full-stack app called \"Treinador de Corrida VDOT & VO2Max\".\n\n\n**Building Interactive Dashboard**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a dynamic dashboard with calculators and predictors. I\'m building a VO2Max/Test Calculator accepting inputs like Cooper test data, 2400m test data, or VDOT pace for instant VO2Max, VDOT, heart rate zones (Karvonen), and pace zone calculations. Furthermore, a Performance Predictor interpolates race time equivalencies across various distances from an input time, all with a smart chart for visualization. Finally, there will be a HR Zones Calculator using the Karvonen formula.\n\n\n**Implementing Pace Zones**\n\nI\'m now implementing VDOT-based pace zone calculations. I\'ll include Easy, Marathon, Threshold, Interval, and Repetition paces directly in the system for training, using formulas or a lookup table. I\'m also planning to build an interactive 8-week training plan generator, with a 3:1 load/recovery cycle, considering user level, target goals, and adherence to safe volume and intensity limits.\nI\'m ensuring beginner plans avoid high intensity and that interval volume is capped at 8% weekly.\n\n**Integrating AI Coach**\n\nI am now focusing on integrating the AI Coach. It will be a chat-based assistant, a \"Treinador de Corrida IA\". This Coach will offer contextual advice and be powered by Gemini. This feature will have full plan and test evaluations, explaining overtraining, and prescribing plans, or active recovery, all while maintaining the specified safety constraints.\n\n\n**Adding Active Context**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating active calculator state into the AI Coach\'s awareness. The AI can now converse about the user\'s current VDOT, heart rate zones, or training plan and modify them, building upon our interactive dashboard. The design will be adjusted to allow the AI to actively utilize the app\'s internal state.\n\n\n**Defining Full-Stack Implementation**\n\nI\'m now determining the implementation details, and am opting for a full-stack approach. This leverages the server to utilize the `@google/genai` library, which is better than the client-side approach, where the `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY` is available. Now I\'m listing the directory files and am checking `metadata.json` to configure the app\'s name to `\"Treinador de Corrida VDOT & VO2Max\"`.\n\n\n**Refining App Name**\n\nI\'ve decided to refine the app\'s name to `\"Calculadora de Corrida e Treinador VDOT\"`, maintaining a focus on clarity and descriptive accuracy. It succinctly captures the app\'s core functions: the VDOT calculator, and the running coach aspects. It is going to be simple, but elegant and modern with high contrast.\n\n\n**Implementing Formulas**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core formulas for the calculators. I\'m focusing on the Cooper test, 2400m test, and VDOT calculations, and am meticulously transcribing them in TypeScript. The intention is to make these calculations available both client-side for the calculator, and server-side to feed context to the AI Coach. It\'s key to ensure precision.\n\n\n**Calculating Race Times**\n\nI\'m now integrating the VDOT formula. I\'ve successfully transcribed the calculation into TypeScript, and it\'s producing precise VDOT values consistent with Daniels\' tables. Now, I\'m focusing on its application for predicting race times, using VDOT as a key input. I am looking into how to use this VDOT calculation in reverse.\n\n\n**Predicting Race Times**\n\nI\'m now implementing a binary search algorithm in TypeScript to predict race times, given a target VDOT and distance. I\'m focusing on ensuring its accuracy for the binary search, effectively solving for the time where the calculated VDOT matches the input VDOT.\n\n\n**Deriving Pace Velocities**\n\nI\'m now deriving pace velocities from VDOT, starting with Easy pace. I\'ve formulated a quadratic equation to calculate Easy pace velocity based on 65% of VDOT, using the Jack Daniels formula. I\'m moving forward with similar calculations for Marathon, Threshold, Interval, and Repetition paces, which I am transcribing into TypeScript.\n\n\n**Deriving Pace Ranges**\n\nI\'m now deriving pace ranges for different training zones based on VDOT percentages. I\'ve successfully calculated the Easy pace using the quadratic formula, and validated its precision. I\'m expanding the formula to determine the Marathon, Threshold, and Interval pace ranges based on the specified percentages of VDOT. This includes transcribing percentage ranges for each pace category.\n\n\n**Validating Pace Calculations**\n\nI\'m now validating the accuracy of pace calculations, and I\'ve confirmed the implementation of the formula is producing consistent and precise pace outputs. I\'ve re-examined the code and confirmed that the output produces correct calculations for all the paces - Easy, Marathon, Threshold, Interval, and Repetition. The repetition pace uses the calculated average, so I\'m happy with its performance. I\'m satisfied that it\'s robust, and ready for integration.\n\n\n**Defining User Interaction**\n\nI\'m now defining the expected user interactions, starting with specific triggers and their corresponding actions. I\'ve categorized them as commands, and outlined how to translate each into appropriate actions within the application. For example, \"avaliar VO2m\303\241x\" triggers a full explanation of different VO2Max test methods, including the Cooper test, 2400m test, and VDOT-based estimations. I\'m focusing on providing clear and helpful responses.\n\n\n**Defining User Triggers**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the user interaction flow with the AI Coach, mapping specific triggers to corresponding actions. I\'ve detailed key commands, such as time predictions, and also recovery advice based on user inputs. I\'m prioritizing providing context-aware advice, and ensuring beginner plans avoid high intensity and that interval volume is capped at 8% weekly, always highlighting the difference between volume and intensity.\n\n\n**Structuring Core Modules**\n\nI\'m now structuring the core modules of the application, focusing on `/src/types.ts` for defining data types like workout data and messages. In `/src/lib/vdot.ts`, I\'m developing the VDOT formula implementations, including the automatic 8-week plan generator with the Daniels formula and Karvonen heart rate calculation. A key goal is `generateTrainingPlan(vdot: number, level: \'beginner\' | \'intermediate\' | \'advanced\', weeklyVolume: number, frequency: number)` functionality. I\'m focusing on user levels to avoid high intensity for beginners, capping interval volume at 8% weekly, and I\'m mindful of the volume and intensity limitations.\n\n\n**Designing Training Plan Features**\n\nI\'m now designing an 8-week, periodized running plan, adhering to specific constraints. The plan\'s structure will follow a 3-week build and 1-week recovery cycle. I\'m focusing on ensuring beginners have low-intensity plans, and all plans adhere to a maximum interval volume of 8% of the weekly volume, highlighting volume and intensity differences.\n\n\n**Refining Server Integration**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the server integration. I\'m checking the `package.json` scripts, and will modify them for full-stack functionality. The plan is to configure \"dev\" to run `server.ts` with `tsx`, \"build\" to compile server code with `esbuild` after a Vite build, and \"start\" to run the compiled server code. This includes gracefully handling missing API keys on the server-side, and now I\'m thinking of creating a custom `server.ts`.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will first update `metadata.json` to set the descriptive name and details of the app: `\"Calculadora de Corrida e Treinador VDOT\"`, describing it as a running calculator and periodized training coach following the Jack Daniels VDOT oxygen cost model."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 37
      nanos: 585252139
    }
