  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Inspecting Immersive UI**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the dark \"Immersive UI\" styles within `src/index.css`. The plan is to see how fast I can modify the base stylesheet and then refactor `src/App.tsx` and all tab components to support the new styling. A key insight: I can adjust styling keywords when modifying the components. For now, I\'m inspecting and writing.\n\n\n**Refining Base Styles**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into `src/index.css`, with a goal of constructing a high-quality base stylesheet. I\'m focusing on redefining background, card, input, and select styles to ensure robust color support. Also, I\'m examining Tailwind v4 to see how custom properties can be declared and used with an `@theme` block to establish custom colors, and redirect colors where possible.\n\n\n**Mapping Tailwind Colors**\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'ve got a breakthrough! I realized I can remap Tailwind\'s default colors (like `gray` and `indigo`) within `src/index.css`\'s `@theme` section. This way, the whole application will instantly adopt the Immersive UI dark cyber theme without requiring tedious, file-by-file changes. I\'m imagining the immediate effects on elements like `bg-white`, `border-gray-100`, `text-gray-800`, `bg-indigo-600`, and `text-indigo-200`!\n\n\n**Defining Color Remapping**\n\nI\'m now nailing down the color remapping strategy for Tailwind. I\'m focusing on defining how the existing `bg-white`, `text-gray-800`, and `border-gray-100` classes will transform within the dark Immersive UI. The plan is to set `bg-white` to a dark slate, use a neon orange for `indigo`, dark outlines for `border-gray-100`, and light gray for fonts. This approach will be elegant and safe, keeping the original structure.\n\n\n**Implementing Dark Mode Styles**\n\nI\'m now making solid progress. I\'ve successfully redefined `--color-white` to `#121212`, which immediately updates `bg-white` classes. Also, I\'m now drafting precise CSS for the `body` and elements such as `select`, `input`, and `textarea`. I\'m going to override the styling within the theme definition, mapping `Emerald`, `Amber`, and `Rose` to custom colors. This should deliver a cohesive \"Immersive UI\" aesthetic. I will set the body to background `#050505`, color `#E0E0E0`, and custom fonts, and make the textboxes sleek with dark backgrounds and borders, and focus in orange.\n\n\n**Analyzing Color Variables**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining custom color variables for the \"Immersive UI\" dark theme. I\'m focusing on incorporating a primary accent color (`#FF4E00`), specifically mapping it to Tailwind\'s indigo shades (50-950) and a custom variable for easy access. I\'m keen on examining how the existing elements (header, calculator bar) respond to the remapping. I am also checking the `src/index.css` structure.\n\n\n**Considering Visual Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now thinking about integrating a glowing circle visualization for the Cooper/2400m test dial, inspired by the design HTML. Specifically, I\'m examining how to add this widget to `src/components/CalculatorTab.tsx` to match the left column layout. The current layout has a VO2 Max dial mentioned, which I can use to inspire me!\n\n\n**Integrating Visual Widget**\n\nI\'m now integrating the captivating circular VO2 Max dial into `CalculatorTab.tsx`. It would be a stellar addition to either the \"Active Fitness Dashboard\" or \"Runner Profile Controls\" sections. The plan is to strategically place the dial element within the current layout, likely by repurposing an existing section. I need to make sure this fits the dark theme.\n\n\n"
