    thinking: "**Analyzing the Dashboard**\n\nOkay, I\'m now zeroing in on the Capacity Dashboard within `CalculatorTab.tsx`. I\'m replacing the `active-fitness-banner` with the dial drawing, classification, and gradient styling. The goal is to make it visually striking and informative, leveraging a deep, dark gradient for a high-performance feel.\n\n\n**Implementing the Dial**\n\nI\'ve successfully integrated the circular VO2 Max dial SVG and its associated displays for VDOT and FCM into the Capacity Dashboard. I\'ve ensured its style exactly matches the mockup\'s \"Capacidade Aer\303\263bica\" component, including the gradient and border. This dial is now interactive and visually represents the runner\'s VO2 Max value.\n\n\n**Defining Global Dark Mode**\n\nOkay, I\'m now delving into the global styling within `src/index.css` to achieve the desired dark, cyberpunk aesthetic. I\'m focusing on overriding light-colored styles and forms to ensure visual depth. The plan is to create custom global rules, such as replacing `bg-white` with `#121212` and a `border-white/5` border. I\'m experimenting with redefining parameters using Tailwind\'s `@theme` block and custom utility classes, aiming for precise styling control. I plan on implementing Tailwind CSS v4 variables with standard overrides for styling, starting with these global theme defaults: `--color-bg-deep`, `--color-bg-card`, `--color-accent`, `--color-text-light`, and `--color-border-card`.\n\n\n**Exploring Tailwind\'s `@theme`**\n\nI\'ve discovered the immense power of Tailwind\'s `@theme` directive within `src/index.css`, as it enables automatic cascading of changes to Tailwind imports. I am confirming that I can define custom variables using Tailwind CSS v4 in my theme definitions. I am reviewing the code to get inspiration for styling colors.\n\n\n**Refining Color Palette**\n\nI\'m now carefully curating the color palette for the dark mode theme. I\'ve defined a series of custom gray scale variables within the theme, ranging from `#202020` to `#ffffff`, and I\'m updating components. The strategy is to ensure consistent background and text colors. I\'m excited because I\'m thinking I can map `--color-white` to `#121212`, which should make components with `bg-white` look perfect. I\'m making the `body`, `input`, `select` and `textarea` elements as well for a clean look.\n\n\n**Styling Input Elements**\n\nI\'ve tackled the styling for form elements to match the dark theme, using custom CSS to ensure consistency and a cohesive look across input fields, selections, and other form containers. This ensures the UI is immersive and uniform. The focus is now shifting to reviewing and potentially updating the header in `src/App.tsx`.\n\n\n**Adjusting the App Header**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the app header styling in `src/App.tsx`, and I\'m updating the existing title and subtitle. I\'m using the provided code snippet as a template to enhance the visual presentation. I\'ll need to adapt the colors, fonts, and sizes to align with the new dark theme\'s aesthetic. I\'m also planning to replace the current text style with an uppercase, bold, and modern look.\n\n\n**Refining the App Header**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Immersive UI design mockup\'s header into `src/App.tsx`. The plan involves wrapping the header in a styled `<header>` tag with a dark background and border, and adding a neon-styled logo. I\'ll maintain the current functional elements (tabs, runner stats) while giving the header a high-performance cyberpunk aesthetic. I\'m keen on making the header appear as visually striking as the dashboard!\n\n\n\n**Integrating the Header**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the proposed header enhancements into `src/App.tsx`. I\'ll replace the existing header section (lines 58-150) with the new layout, including the title, subtitle, and tab list, to establish the header\'s consistent look and feel. I am excited to see how this enhances the immersive experience.\n\n\n"
