  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining the Instruction**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed lines 75-112 as the declaration site for `systemInstruction`. My focus is now on crafting a robust instruction, which will guide the user onboarding. I\'m exploring interactive custom rules, and I\'ve started the instruction with a markdown block. I\'ll translate the instruction, which begins: \"Voc\303\252 \303\251 o \'Treinador...\"\n\n\n**Defining the Core Persona**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in defining the core persona for this instruction set. The persona will be a friendly, motivational, highly technical, elite running coach and physiologist. It\'s crucial for the user to be guided through the registration process and training plans based on Jack Daniels\' VDOT methodology, Karvonen heart rate zones, and sports safety. A \"Golden Rule\" is absolutely critical: I can\'t generate any training data before gathering eight key pieces of user information.\n\n\n**Structuring the Questionnaire**\n\nI\'m structuring the required user questionnaire to capture key data points. Specifically, I\'m focusing on defining mandatory fields. I must obtain the user\'s running experience level, training days per week, and primary goal (e.g., 5k completion, half-marathon). If *any* of this information is missing, I will have to refrain from generating training plans until the data is submitted, as I\'m developing a step-by-step interactive onboarding process. The process starts with a welcome message, followed by friendly, concise prompts for each required field.\n\n\n**Formulating the Questions**\n\nI\'m now writing and refining the specific questions for the questionnaire. I\'m focusing on clarity and user-friendliness to elicit accurate responses. I\'m developing a method to explain complex concepts like Heart Rate Max (FCm\303\241x) and Resting Heart Rate (FCR) in an accessible way. I will provide users with explanations and estimations. I\'m also ensuring clear and concise options for the running experience level.\n\n\n**Crafting the Questionnaire**\n\nI\'m currently crafting the questionnaire, adding fields for current weekly running volume in kilometers and consecutive active weeks. I\'ll provide clear explanations, including an option for users who don\'t have recent race times, offering field tests like the 12-minute Cooper Test or the 2400m test. I will be sure to address that the user will need to provide data from a field test after the questionnaire to complete the onboarding. I\'m also including options for available training days per week, the user\'s primary goal (e.g., completing a 5k), and an optional field for injury history.\n\n\n**Addressing Missing Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on handling missing data and estimated values. If a user doesn\'t know their FCm\303\241x, FCR, or best race time, I\'ll use the suggested alternatives and inform them about the imprecision respectfully. For instance, I\'ll ask, \"Since you don\'t know your FCm\303\241x, I\'ll use the estimate of [220-age or 226-age] bpm. Is that okay?\"\n\nI\'ll present a clean, organized summary of *all* collected parameters in a bulleted list, including: Age, Biological Sex, Weight, FCm\303\241x, FCR, Experience, Current Volume, Active Weeks, Recent Best Time (or pending field test), Training Days/Week, Goal, and Injury History. I\'ll prompt the user for confirmation after presenting the summary.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Confirmation**\n\nI\'m now concluding the summary with a clear confirmation question: \"Is the information above correct? Can we proceed to perform the physiological calculations and generate your complete plan?\"\nI\'m ensuring the data summary includes any reported injury history or \"No history provided\".\n\n\\n\\n\n**Generating the Training Data**\n\nI\'m ready to generate all training data once the user confirms their information. I will calculate the Karvonen heart rate zones, providing exact thresholds in bpm: Easy, Threshold, Interval, and Repetition. Then I\'ll generate the Pace Table, and training model.\n\n\n**Calculating the Running Paces**\n\nI\'m now focused on deriving the VDOT pace table based on the user\'s race time. I\'ll translate the race time to an estimated VDOT, mapping this to training paces: Easy, Threshold, Interval, and Repetition. I\'ll present an explicit table with precise training paces (e.g., E: 5:45/km, T: 5:00/km).\n\n\n**Adding Safety Considerations**\n\nI\'m now incorporating important safety rules. Specifically, the volume of Interval (I) or Repetition (R) pace runs will be limited to a maximum of 8% of the weekly running volume. I\'ll also add a rule that volume and intensity should never increase by more than 10% in the same week. I\'ll include a critical warning to stop running immediately if experiencing any acute or persistent pain in shins, knees, or heels.\n\n\n**Refining the Output**\n\nI\'m now integrating all aspects of data generation. I\'m focusing on crafting a comprehensive training plan that considers age, sex, FCm\303\241x (real or estimated), resting heart rate (or a default of 60 bpm), best race time (or field test), experience level, available training days, and the user\'s running goals. I must present a summary of the data and request confirmation before generating calculations. I\'ll translate the instruction, which begins: \"Voc\303\252 \303\251 o \'Treinador...\"\n\n\n**Integrating Safety Measures**\n\nI\'m now integrating crucial safety guidelines. I\'ll translate the instruction, which begins: \"Voc\303\252 \303\251 o \'Treinador...\" I\'m restricting interval and repetition run volume to a maximum of 8% of the weekly running volume. A 10% limit on combined volume and intensity increases is a must, and I\'ll include a critical warning to stop if there\'s any pain. I\'m focusing on crafting a full training plan.\n\n\n**Expanding the Training Plan**\n\nI\'m now expanding the training plan to include performance predictions using VDOT equivalencies for various distances. I\'ll translate the instruction, which begins: \"Voc\303\252 \303\251 o \'Treinador...\" I\'m also preparing for recommendations about rest and injury prevention, proactively addressing overtraining signs. I\'ll be sure to include warnings about overtraining, and proactively asking about physical pains.\n\n\n**Adding Recovery Recommendations**\n\nI\'m now integrating recommendations for immediate volume adjustments or rest days if the user reports overtraining signs, aiming for a 20-30% volume reduction or extra rest. I\'m also preparing an active recovery protocol, covering Z1 easy runs, soft myofascial release, hydration, and sleep, and I\'ll translate the instruction, which begins: \"Voc\303\252 \303\251 o \'Treinador...\"\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now edit `/server.ts` to implement the Virtual running coach system instructions with the interactive guided onboarding flow (Etapa de Cadastro Guiado) and the golden security and data collection rules."
